\section{Termination, Soundness and Completeness }\label{sec:correct}


We show the procedure terminates in deterministic exponential time (\S\ref{ss:term}) and is sound and
complete for all non-punctual TCTL (\S\ref{ss:sound}). Throughout, $\varphi$ is the input, $n=|\FL(\varphi)|$, and $M$ the largest constant in its intervals.

\subsection{Termination and complexity}\label{ss:term}

First, we prove the procedure terminates and it is \textsc{ExpTime}-complete. To do so, we first bound the number of distinct nodes the procedure can generate.
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:finiteness}
The number of distinct nodes the procedure can generate is bounded above by $2^{O(n)}\cdot M^{O(n^2)}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The propositional components $\Gamma,\Ctx$ range over subsets of $\FL(\varphi)$, hence
$2^{O(n)}$ choices each; the clock map $\obligations$ over $2^{O(n)}$ assignments and $D$ over $2^{O(n)}$ choices. The cover rule allocates one
formula-clock per timed subformula, bounded by $O(n)$. A canonical $\cong_M$-zone over $K$
clocks is a $(K{+}1)^2$ DBM whose finite entries lie in $\{-M,\dots,M\}\times\{<,\le\}$,
giving $M^{O(K^2)}\le M^{O(n^2)}$ distinct zones. Multiplying the components yields the
bound.
\end{proof}



Now we can prove the main result of this section.



\begin{theorem}\label{thm:complexity}
Satisfiability of non-punctual TCTL is \textsc{ExpTime}-complete, and the tableau
decides it in time $2^{\mathrm{poly}(n)}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $m=2^{O(n)}\cdot M^{O(n^2)}$ be the node bound of Lemma~\ref{lem:finiteness}; since the
encoding of $M$ occupies at most $n$ input symbols, $\log m=O(n)+O(n^2)\log M=\mathrm{poly}(n)$.
Nodes are drawn from a set of size $m$, so the memoised AND--OR graph has at most $m^2$ edges;
by the pigeonhole principle every infinite branch repeats a node, where the loop rule adds a
back-edge or closes, and the construction halts after at most $m$ expansions. Memoisation ensures each node is expanded at most once, avoiding the doubly exponential
blow-up of the naive ancestor-only unfolding.
%
%Memoisation is
%essential rather than an optimisation: with ancestor-only loop detection the same graph unfolds
%to a doubly-exponential tree.

The satisfaction sets are computable within this budget. Each
$\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$ is a least ($\mathbf{U}$) or greatest ($\mathbf{R}$) fixpoint of a monotone map on unions of $\cong_M$-zones, which are at most $M^{O(n^2)}$ in number, thus finite, so by Knaster--Tarski the
fixpoint exists and monotone iteration reaches it in as many steps, each a polynomial number of
DBM operations, with a factor $O(n)$ over $\FL(\varphi)$.


A successful marking is then found by the standard elimination procedure for
AND--OR tableaux~\cite{Clarke99,tableau_ctl}: repeatedly delete every closed node,
every OR-node whose children are all deleted, every AND-node with a deleted
child, every node from which no surviving path reaches a time-progressing loop,
and every node from which no surviving path takes the $(\text{divergence})$ branch of
an until pursuit active there. The first three tests are local; the last two are
reachability queries over the surviving subgraph, each decidable in time
polynomial in $m$, and it is sound to recompute them each round because deletion
only ever removes paths, never creates them. The two queries enforce conditions
(ii) and (i) of \S\ref{ss:loop} respectively. Each round deletes at least one
node, so at most $m$ rounds suffice, and $\varphi$ is satisfiable iff the root
survives. The procedure therefore runs in $2^{\mathrm{poly}(n)}$.




%
%It remains to decide existence of a successful marking. A marking retains all AND-children, so
%goodness of every infinite path is a universal condition over the AND-branching: an AND-fork into one good and one bad cycle is rejected. Existence is therefore
%the winning condition of a two-player game on the finite graph --- the proponent resolving the
%OR-choices, the opponent the AND-branches and the path --- won by the proponent iff every play
%is an accepting lasso, every eventuality fulfilled and time-progressing by
%Lemma~\ref{lem:nonzeno}. This is a Streett condition with $k=O(n)$ pairs, one per eventuality
%and one per delay-bounding clock, solved by the standard nested-fixpoint algorithm in
%$m^{O(k)}=2^{O(k\log m)}$, singly exponential in $n$.
%
%Hardness is inherited: fixing every interval to $[0,\infty)$ the procedure decides CTL
%satisfiability, which is \textsc{ExpTime}-hard~\cite{Clarke99}.
\end{proof}










\subsection{Soundness and Completeness}\label{ss:sound}

We now prove the procedure is sound, i.e., that if the tableau succeeds on $\varphi$, then $\varphi$ is \SAT, and complete, i.e., that if $\varphi$ is \SAT, then the tableau succeeds on $\varphi$. These results are supported by the following lemmas. 

%
%By finiteness a successful marking $\mathcal{M}$ is a finite AND--OR structure whose infinite paths are
%lassos $\pi_{\mathsf{pref}}\cdot\pi_{\mathsf{loop}}^\omega$ with an accepting loop~\cite{Clarke99}. From
%$\mathcal{M}$ we build a witness TTS $\mathcal{T}$, then verify it by induction. Three lemmas support the
%construction: one makes the zones realisable as delays, one reconciles the discrete induction with the dense
%semantics, and one guarantees time divergence.
%

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:feasible}
Let $N_0,\dots,N_k$ be a finite path of $\mathcal{M}$ with non-empty zones. For every
$\nu_k\in Z_k$ there are $\nu_0\in Z_0,\dots,\nu_{k-1}\in Z_{k-1}$ and positive delays
realising every step.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Each operation used along a path (intersection and guards,
reset, time-successor and
$\cong_M$-canonicalisation) admits back-propagation, non-empty
precisely because the successor valuation lies in its image~\cite{Bouyer05TimedAutomata}; threading
backward from $\nu_k$ gives the assignment.
\end{proof}

%\begin{lemma}[Feasibility]\label{lem:feasible}
%Along any finite path of $\mathcal{M}$ whose nodes carry non-empty zones, there is a consistent assignment of
%clock valuations and delays realising every time-elapse and reset step.
%\end{lemma}
%\begin{proof}
%The zone operations the path uses -- intersection, single-clock guard $x\in\kI$, reset $Z[R{:=}0]$,
%time-successor $\uparrow Z$, and $\cong_M$-canonicalisation -- each preserve non-emptiness and, given a
%representative of the successor zone, admit a compatible representative of the predecessor zone (the standard
%DBM back-propagation~\cite{AlurDill1994,Bouyer05TimedAutomata}). Threading representatives from the end of the
%path backward yields valuations and positive delays consistent with every step.
%\end{proof}
%
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:stability}
Along a delay step $(s,\nu)\xrightarrow{t}(s,\nu+t)$ that crosses no integer hyperplane $x=b$ ($b\le M$), the
truth of every subformula of $\varphi$ is constant over the relatively-open segment $(s,\nu+t')$,
$t'\in(0,t)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Crossing no hyperplane $x=b$ with $b\le M$, no clock crosses an integer at or below $M$, so
no integer part changes and no fractional ordering wraps; clocks already above $M$ are
identified by extrapolation and their exact values are immaterial. All $(s,\nu+t')$
therefore lie in one clock region, and TCTL truth is constant on a
region~\cite{AlurCourcoubetisDill1990,Bouyer05TimedAutomata}.
\end{proof}
\noindent
The endpoints of the segment, where a clock may sit on a hyperplane, are made into nodes of
their own by the integer split of \ruleref{Delay}, and are matched against the strictness
flags of the interval bounds. 

%\begin{proof}
%Crossing no such hyperplane, no clock changes integer part below $M$ and no fractional ordering wraps, so all
%$(s,\nu+t')$ lie in one clock region; TCTL truth is constant on a region (the region
%theorem~\cite{AlurCourcoubetisDill1990,Bouyer05TimedAutomata}). The integer-split of successor generation
%makes the segment endpoints, where a clock may sit on a hyperplane, into nodes, handled by the strictness
%flags of the interval bounds. Diagonal-freeness and integer endpoints suffice; non-punctuality is not needed
%here.
%\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:nonzeno}
A candidate loop $N_i \equiv N_j$ has a non-Zeno unfolding iff it is {time-progressing}: it admits positive total elapsed time and every clock is either reset on the segment, replenishing its budget,
or extrapolated above $M$, hence unbounded.
When it is, a run exists in which every iteration elapses the same positive amount%, i.e. $\exists \delta >0$ such that every iteration elapses time $\delta$
.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) If some clock $x$ is not reset on the segment yet keeps a finite bound $c\le M$, then along
the loop $x$ only grows while staying $\le c$, so the total delay over all iterations is at most
$c-\nu(x)<\infty$ and every unfolding is Zeno; likewise if the loop can elapse only zero time. ($\Leftarrow$) Suppose the loop elapses positive time. Lemma~\ref{lem:feasible} yields a
traversal over the loop with some $\delta>0$. Since $N_i\equiv N_j$, no rule distinguishes
the two visits and the traversal is repeated; no budget depletes either, each clock being
reset on the segment or extrapolated above $M$. Hence $\delta$ recurs at every iteration and
$\sum_i t_i\ge\sum_i\delta=\infty$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}%[Adequacy of $\Sat$]
    \label{lem:sat}
Let $\mu$ be demanded at a node $N$ under context $\Ctx$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] Let $\nu\in Z_N$. If $\nu\in\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$, then $\mathcal{T},(s_N,\nu)\models\mu$, where
  $\mathcal{T}$ is the model read off the pursuit branches of a successful marking and
  $s_N$ the location of $N$.
\item[(ii)] If $\mathcal{T}',(s,\nu)\models\mu$ for a model $\mathcal{T}'$ meeting $\Ctx$ at
  $(s,\nu)$, then $\nu\in\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}

By construction, $\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$ tracks the path quantifier of \eqref{eq:until}--\eqref{eq:release}


A valuation lies in $\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$ if it lies in a base child of \ruleref{Uunf} or \ruleref{Runf}, or in
a recursive child whose deferred successors lie in $\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$, one successor when
$\mathrm{Q}=\mathrm{E}$ and all of them when $\mathrm{Q}=\mathrm{A}$. This is
\eqref{eq:until}--\eqref{eq:release} read one step at a time, the path quantifier tracked by
where the residual travels, $\EX\mu^\bullet$ into $\Gamma$ and $\AX\mu^\bullet$ into $\Ctx$. By
construction, $\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$ is the least fixpoint of $F$ for an until and the greatest
for a release, and Lemma~\ref{lem:stability} lifts each step from the nodes to the dense
positions between them.


$\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$ is a fixpoint of the pursuit unfolding, and that unfolding mirrors the
semantic clause one step at a time, relative to $\Ctx$. The quantifiers of \eqref{eq:sat_u}--\eqref{eq:sat_r} are matched by the $\EX/\AX$-successors of the pursuit and the operator fixes which fixpoint of that equation is
taken: least for until, so membership means reaching a stop in finitely many steps; greatest
for release, so membership means never violating the obligation. Using Lemma~\ref{lem:stability}, each step is lifted from the nodes to the dense positions between them. Accordingly:


\textit{(i)}  The runs of $\mathcal{T}$ from $(s_N,\nu)$ correspond to the pursuit paths from $N$: the discrete
edges of $\mathcal{T}$ are the pursuits' $\EX/\AX$-successors, while the discharge child of
\ruleref{Cov} drops $\mu$ and contributes no edge. Now $\nu\in\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$ means $\nu$ satisfies the fixpoint equation, hence entered the iteration at some stage via a specific branch of the pursuit; tracing that back selects a
path through the pursuit graph, which the correspondence turns into a run of $\mathcal{T}$. For an until the least fixpoint bounds the unwinding, so the run
reaches a stop in finitely many steps; for a release the greatest fixpoint means it never
violates the obligation. For a universal until the deadline $x_\mu\le\sup\kI$ carried by
$\Inv(N)$ caps every branch inside the window, so no run drifts past it; a release lapses
instead.

\textit{(ii)} By hypothesis the runs of $\mathcal{T}'$ from $(s,\nu)$ satisfy
\eqref{eq:until}--\eqref{eq:release}, and each projects onto a pursuit path whose valuations
lie in the corresponding zones. An until reaches its witness in finitely many discrete steps,
since $\mathcal{T}'$ is non-Zeno and cannot accumulate infinitely many within the window, so
$\nu$ enters the iteration at a finite stage and lies in the least fixpoint. A release yields a
post-fixpoint, every in-window position carrying $\psi$ or having been released earlier by
$\varphi$, and the greatest fixpoint contains it.
\end{proof}

\noindent
Now we can establish that the tableau procedure is sound for non-punctual TCTL.

\begin{theorem}%[Soundness]
    \label{thm:soundness}
The tableau procedure is sound.
\end{theorem}
%with induced TTS $\mathcal{T}$
\begin{proof}
Take a successful marking $\mathcal{M}$. We read off a witness TA $\mathcal{A}$ as follows.
Its locations are the nodes of $\mathcal{M}$, each labelled by the atoms in its $\Gamma$ and
carrying $\Inv(N)$ as its invariant, which is what forces time forward into demanded windows.
Its discrete edges are the AND-children retained by $\mathcal{M}$, each guarded by
$Z_N\cap\Inv(N)$ for its target $N$: every $\EX\psi$ obtains a successor satisfying $\psi$, with the corresponding
formula-clocks reset on the edge, and every node's context $\Ctx$ is imposed on all its successors, so
the universal obligations constrain the whole branching. Delays are supplied by \ruleref{Delay}, which generates no edges: the location is unchanged
and only the zone advances, so a chain of such steps is one continuous elapse through
successive zones of a single location. Lemma~\ref{lem:feasible} yields valuations and positive
delays consistent with every time-elapse and reset step along a path.

$\mathcal{A}$ is non-blocking. Its only invariants are the until deadlines in $\Inv(N)$,
registered by \ruleref{Cov} when it launches the pursuit of a universal until and retired by
the $(\text{stop})$ branch of \ruleref{Uunf}. The cap inherits the strictness of $\kI$, and so
does the guard $x_\mu\in\kI$ of that same stop branch: the stop edge is enabled over exactly
the range the invariant permits the location to be occupied, and no configuration is left with
time blocked and no move available.

Let $\mathcal{T}$ be the TTS induced by $\mathcal{A}$. We now show that every run of $\mathcal{T}$ is non-Zeno. Since $\mathcal{A}$ is finite, an infinite run traverses cycles of $\mathcal{A}$ infinitely often, and these are the back-edges of $\mathcal{M}$, which the loop rule adds
only to accepting, hence time-progressing, loops. By Lemma~\ref{lem:nonzeno} each
traversal elapses a fixed $\delta>0$, so the delays sum to $\infty$.

Now we can show that $\mathcal{T},(s_0,\nu_0)\models\varphi$ by induction on $\varphi$. Booleans follow
from saturation of $\Gamma$ and closure of contradictory nodes, $\EX\psi$ and $\AX\psi$ from
the construction of the edges and the context. For a timed modality $\mu$ demanded at $N$ the
cover rule succeeded, so $Z_N\subseteq\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:sat}(i) gives
$\mathcal{T},(s_N,\nu)\models\mu$ for every $\nu\in Z_N$, ensuring one zone discharges otherwise uncountably many onsets of $\mu$ in the dense semantics.
Lemma~\ref{lem:stability} lifts each conclusion from the nodes to the dense positions between
them, and since every run of $\mathcal{T}$ is non-Zeno the modalities hold over
$\mathrm{Runs}^\infty$.%, as the semantics requires.
\end{proof}

\noindent Finally, we prove that the tableau procedure is complete for non-punctual TCTL.
\begin{theorem}%[Completeness]
    \label{thm:completeness}
    The tableau procedure is complete.
%If $\varphi$ is satisfiable, then the tableau succeeds on $\varphi$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}



Let $\mathcal{T},(s_0,\nu_0)\models\varphi$ with $\mathcal{T}$ non-Zeno. We construct a
successful marking $\mathcal{M}$ by reading it off $\mathcal{T}$, maintaining at each node a
configuration of $\mathcal{T}$ whose valuation lies in the node's zone and which satisfies its
$\Gamma$ and $\Ctx$.



At an OR-node a consistent child exists in each case. For $(\vee)$, $\mathcal{T}$ satisfies a
disjunct. For \ruleref{Covstar}, $\mathcal{T},(s,\nu)\models\mu$ gives
$\nu\in\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:sat}(ii), so $\nu$ lies in some component of
$Z\cap\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$. For \ruleref{Uunf}, either the witness is at the current position
with $x_\mu\in\kI$, giving stop, or it is strictly later, so $\varphi$ holds now and go
applies. For \ruleref{Runf}, the before-part's continue child demands nothing, and in the
window $\psi$ holds --- no earlier $\varphi$ has released $\mu^\bullet$, or it would not still
be carried.

%, with a pursuit $\mu^\bullet$ carried only while $\mathcal{T}$ has not yetdischarged $\mu$.

%
%
%Let $\mathcal{T},(s_0,\nu_0)\models\varphi$ with $\mathcal{T}$ non-Zeno. We now construct a successful marking off of $\mathcal{M}$, maintaining at each node a configuration of $\mathcal{T}$ that satisfies the node's $\Gamma$ within its zone.  
%%
%For each OR-node, we selct the child consistent with $\mathcal{T}$ at the current configuration; one exists because $\mathcal{T}$ satisfies the disjunction, the unfolded fixpoint, or lies in some component of $Z\cap\Sat(\mu)$. 
%
%we read a successful marking off it,
%maintaining at each node a configuration of $\mathcal{T}$ that satisfies the node's $\Gamma$ within its zone.



At an OR-node -- a $(\vee)$, a pursuit branch, or a $(\mathrm{Chk}^{*})$ refinement -- select the child
consistent with $\mathcal{T}$ at the current configuration; one exists because $\mathcal{T}$ satisfies the
disjunction, the unfolded fixpoint, or lies in some component of $Z\cap\Sat(\mu)$. At a successor-generation
(AND) node, realise {every} demanded $\EX\psi$ by a discrete successor of $\mathcal{T}$ satisfying
$\psi$, and recurse on {all} of them; the context $\Ctx$ holds at each because $\mathcal{T}$ meets its
$\AX$-obligations. Every chosen zone intersection is non-empty, witnessed by the model's valuations, so no
selected node closes.

A timed modality demanded at a configuration is verified on its own check, the formula-clock reset there;
since $\mathcal{T}\models\mu$ at {that} configuration, $\Sat(\mu)$ -- computed under the same context --
covers it (Lemma~\ref{lem:sat}) and the check succeeds. A persistent context re-imposes $\mu$ at a continuum
of onsets, but the single universal test $Z\subseteq\Sat(\mu)$ over the demanding zone decides them together,
no onset inspected on its own. By finiteness every path of the marking eventually repeats a node; because
$\mathcal{T}$ fulfils each until in finite time and is non-Zeno, the repeating cycle is fulfilling and
time-progressing, hence accepting (Lemma~\ref{lem:nonzeno}), and the marking is successful.

This last step -- that a satisfying model projects onto the {finite} symbolic graph as an
{accepting} lasso -- is where non-punctuality is essential. It is the finite-model property of the
fragment~\cite{fsttcs2000-LN}, carried here by the $\cong_M$-extrapolation of
zones~\cite{AlurCourcoubetisDill1990,Bouyer05TimedAutomata} (which handles invariants): non-punctuality makes
the in-window stop set $\cong_M$-stable -- an open window retains a non-empty interior under extrapolation --
so a satisfiable formula admits a model captured, up to $\cong_M$, by finitely many tableau nodes with
fulfilling cycles, and our successful marking {is} such a finite timed automaton. Singular windows forfeit this property, the source of the undecidability of full TCTL
satisfiability~\cite{AlurCourcoubetisDill1990,AlurHenzinger1994ReallyTemporal}. Accordingly our soundness and
termination arguments invoke no property of the intervals; completeness, established here over the
timed-automaton model, reaches the same decidable boundary that La~Torre and Napoli~\cite{fsttcs2000-LN}
reach over dense trees, by a direct construction rather than an automata reduction. (Were the grammar to
admit punctual bounds, the procedure would still run and terminate, but a satisfiable punctual formula may
have no tableau-representable model, so we do not claim completeness there.)
\end{proof}
